1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,472
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:04,972
Episode 28.

3
00:00:04,972 --> 00:00:07,622
The Ice Cube's Hidden Side.

4
00:00:07,622 --> 00:00:10,516
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,516 --> 00:00:14,103
Why can ordinary ice float in liquid water?

6
00:00:14,103 --> 00:00:15,625
Ready?

7
00:00:15,625 --> 00:00:17,730
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,730 --> 00:00:21,793
Leo drops an ice cube into a clear cup of water.

9
00:00:21,793 --> 00:00:26,059
Part of it sticks out, but most of it is below the surface.

10
00:00:26,059 --> 00:00:29,619
It is a very small iceberg, Maya says.

11
00:00:29,619 --> 00:00:34,021
Pip draws a tiny ship on paper and holds it beside the cup.

12
00:00:34,021 --> 00:00:38,057
The captain reports a much bigger object underwater.

13
00:00:38,057 --> 00:00:40,298
Why does the ice float?

14
00:00:40,298 --> 00:00:42,418
Leo asks.

15
00:00:42,418 --> 00:00:45,638
It is solid, so I thought it would sink.

16
00:00:45,638 --> 00:00:51,100
Ms. Park explains that being solid is not enough to decide whether something floats.

17
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:54,769
Ordinary ice is less dense than liquid water.

18
00:00:54,769 --> 00:00:58,124
For the same volume, it has less mass.

19
00:00:58,124 --> 00:01:01,997
They use the idea of two equal-sized boxes.

20
00:01:01,997 --> 00:01:07,255
If one contains more mass packed into that space, it has greater density.

21
00:01:07,255 --> 00:01:13,070
The boxes are only a comparison; water molecules are not little toys in boxes.

22
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:16,942
Maya notices that the cube keeps turning slowly.

23
00:01:16,942 --> 00:01:21,915
She draws the visible part above the line and the larger part below it.

24
00:01:21,915 --> 00:01:26,521
A photograph showing only the top could be misleading, she says.

25
00:01:26,521 --> 00:01:30,896
Leo thinks of seeing only one page of a mystery story.

26
00:01:30,896 --> 00:01:33,872
You might miss most of the evidence.

27
00:01:33,872 --> 00:01:38,926
Pip gives the paper ship a new instruction: Respect what you cannot see.

28
00:01:38,926 --> 00:01:42,948
As the ice melts, the club watches its shape change.

29
00:01:42,948 --> 00:01:46,548
Our iceberg is getting smaller, says Maya.

30
00:01:46,548 --> 00:01:48,858
Pip folds up the ship.

31
00:01:48,858 --> 00:01:53,383
The captain will now explore a much drier place: the bookshelf.

32
00:01:53,383 --> 00:01:56,399
Now, a little more to discover.

33
00:01:56,399 --> 00:02:00,815
Ordinary ice floats in liquid water because it is less dense.

34
00:02:00,815 --> 00:02:05,760
Water expands when it freezes under familiar everyday conditions.

35
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:10,339
Most of a floating ice cube or iceberg stays below the surface.

36
00:02:10,339 --> 00:02:16,059
Density compares mass with volume; size alone does not tell you how dense something is.

37
00:02:16,059 --> 00:02:19,089
Let us hear the main idea again.

38
00:02:19,089 --> 00:02:22,364
Most of the ice cube was underwater.

39
00:02:22,364 --> 00:02:29,769
The club connected floating ice with lower density and learned that the visible top does not show the full size of an object.

40
00:02:29,769 --> 00:02:32,771
Here are three useful expressions.

41
00:02:32,771 --> 00:02:35,815
You can listen, or say them with me.

42
00:02:35,815 --> 00:02:38,749
Most of it is below the surface.

43
00:02:38,749 --> 00:02:41,684
Most of it is below the surface.

44
00:02:41,684 --> 00:02:44,293
We can only see part of it.

45
00:02:44,293 --> 00:02:46,902
We can only see part of it.

46
00:02:46,902 --> 00:02:50,135
Size alone does not tell us everything.

47
00:02:50,135 --> 00:02:53,369
Size alone does not tell us everything.

48
00:02:53,369 --> 00:02:56,236
Remember our listening question?

49
00:02:56,236 --> 00:02:59,823
Why can ordinary ice float in liquid water?

50
00:02:59,823 --> 00:03:02,010
Here is the answer.

51
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:05,747
It is less dense than the surrounding liquid water.

52
00:03:05,747 --> 00:03:09,538
And here is a question just for your imagination.

53
00:03:09,538 --> 00:03:14,239
What other situations show you only a small part of the whole picture?

54
00:03:14,239 --> 00:03:17,404
There is more than one possible answer.

55
00:03:17,404 --> 00:03:20,557
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
